<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<preface>
    <p>
This book is designed as a text for a semester long game development course.
The text is written in PreTeXt, and the exercises are written in GDScript with
the help of the GDPractice addon, originally created by GDQuest, and modified
by the author to make <c>ActiveCode</c> programs that can be run in the textbook.
    </p>
    <p>
The course is meant to cover basic game development concepts for both 2D and 3D games
and game networking capabilities for either 2D or 3D games. The method for learning is
to copy the process of building a game that already exists, and then to remix the game
in order to either stregthen your knowledge of game development tasks or to learn a few
new game development tasks in the process of remixing. This method gives students the
constraints of building particular game types that already exist, while also giving students
agency to pursue the parts of game development that are most interesting or that the student
needs the most practice with.
    </p>
    <p>
    This book has activities that can be done inside this book that involve writing GDScript code.
    In addition, there are descriptions with images of the Godot Game Engine editor that walk the
    student through building parts of a game in the editor.
    </p>
    <p>
    The student is expected to read this book alongside the Godot Editor and to follow along the book
    as it describes the steps. In addition, all of the non-networked games that the student builds
    can be published to GitHub using the <url href="https://github.com/WCU-CS-CooperLab/GamePublish/">GamePublish</url>
    template repository as a template. If you are a student in a class, then this template will be distributed
    to you using the <url href="https://classroom50.org">Classroom50</url> assignment system.
    </p>

</preface>